{"date": "2026-11-09", "post_id": 1508010, "title": "Rigel and the Witch Head Nebula", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-9-rigel-and-the-witch-head-nebula/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Does the blue nebula look like the head of a witch? The nebula is <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/todays-image/witch-head-nebula-orion/\">known popularly</a> as the Witch Head Nebula because, it is said, the nebula's shape resembles a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halloween#/media/File:Halloween_Witch_2011.JPG\">Halloween-style caricature</a> of a witch's head. Exactly how, though, can be a topic of <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-cat-wearing-glasses-and-a-jacket-on-a-rug-nzfEUQrZZGc\">imaginative speculation</a>. What is clear is that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IC_2118\">IC 2118</a> is about 50 <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/\">light-years</a> across and made of gas and dust that points to -- because it has been partly eroded by -- the nearby star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rigel\">Rigel</a>. One of the brighter stars in the constellation Orion, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-april-7-rigel-wide/\">Rigel</a> lies just below the center of the <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/PiotrC?i=ttmxgn\">featured image</a>. The blue color of the Witch Head Nebula and is caused not only by <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-may-8-comet-r3-panstarrs-before-rigel/\">Rigel's intense blue starlight</a> but also because the <a href=\"https://curator.jsc.nasa.gov/dust/\">dust grains</a> scatter blue light more efficiently than red. The same <a href=\"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/atmos/blusky.html\">physical process</a> causes Earth's daytime <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/\">sky to appear blue</a>, although the scatterers in planet <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-atmosphere/earths-atmosphere-a-multi-layered-cake/\">Earth's atmosphere</a> are molecules of <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/nasas-curiosity-rover-finds-biologically-useful-nitrogen-on-mars/\">nitrogen</a> and <a href=\"https://astrobiology.nasa.gov/news/lightning-oxygen-and-life-detection/\">oxygen</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> suddenly Saturn", "credit": "<a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/PiotrC\">Piotr Czerski</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/PiotrC\">Piotr Czerski</a>", "alt": "A starfield filled with red glowing gas is seen surroinging a bright blue star near the center. To the upper right of the star is an unusually shaped blue-tinted nebula.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-9-rigel-and-the-witch-head-nebula/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/WitchHead_Czerski_3000.jpg?w=3000&h=1990&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 9 - Rigel and the Witch Head Nebula</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-9-rigel-and-the-witch-head-nebula/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Does the blue nebula look like the head of a witch? The nebula is known popularly as the Witch Head Nebula because, it is said, the nebula&#039;s shape resembles a Halloween-style caricature of a witch&#039;s head. Exactly how, though, can be a topic of imaginative speculation. What is clear is that IC 2118 is about 50 light-years across and made of gas and dust that points to -- because it has been partly eroded by -- the nearby star Rigel. One of the brighter stars in the constellation Orion, Rigel lies just below the center of the featured image. The blue color of the Witch Head Nebula and is caused not only by Rigel&#039;s intense blue starlight but also because the dust grains scatter blue light more efficiently than red. The same physical process causes Earth&#039;s daytime sky to appear blue, although the scatterers in planet Earth&#039;s atmosphere are molecules of nitrogen and oxygen.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: suddenly Saturn\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"rigel and the witch head nebula\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Rigel and the Witch Head Nebula\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-9-rigel-and-the-witch-head-nebula/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/WitchHead_Czerski_3000.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=1990&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/WitchHead_Czerski_3000.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=1990&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A starfield filled with red glowing gas is seen surroinging a bright blue star near the center. To the upper right of the star is an unusually shaped blue-tinted nebula.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-09T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-04T10:32:17-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Rigel and the Witch Head Nebula\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/WitchHead_Czerski_3000.jpg?w=3000&#038;h=1990&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 9<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/WitchHead_Czerski_3000.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/WitchHead_Czerski_3000.jpg\" alt=\"A starfield filled with red glowing gas is seen surroinging a bright blue star near the center. To the upper right of the star is an unusually shaped blue-tinted nebula.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Rigel and the Witch Head Nebula </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/PiotrC\">Piotr Czerski</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Does the blue nebula look like the head of a witch? The nebula is <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/todays-image/witch-head-nebula-orion/\">known popularly</a> as the Witch Head Nebula because, it is said, the nebula's shape resembles a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halloween#/media/File:Halloween_Witch_2011.JPG\">Halloween-style caricature</a> of a witch's head. Exactly how, though, can be a topic of <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/a-cat-wearing-glasses-and-a-jacket-on-a-rug-nzfEUQrZZGc\">imaginative speculation</a>. What is clear is that <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IC_2118\">IC 2118</a> is about 50 <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/light-year/\">light-years</a> across and made of gas and dust that points to -- because it has been partly eroded by -- the nearby star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rigel\">Rigel</a>. One of the brighter stars in the constellation Orion, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-april-7-rigel-wide/\">Rigel</a> lies just below the center of the <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/PiotrC?i=ttmxgn\">featured image</a>. The blue color of the Witch Head Nebula and is caused not only by <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-may-8-comet-r3-panstarrs-before-rigel/\">Rigel's intense blue starlight</a> but also because the <a href=\"https://curator.jsc.nasa.gov/dust/\">dust grains</a> scatter blue light more efficiently than red. The same <a href=\"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/atmos/blusky.html\">physical process</a> causes Earth's daytime <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/blue-sky/en/\">sky to appear blue</a>, although the scatterers in planet <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-atmosphere/earths-atmosphere-a-multi-layered-cake/\">Earth's atmosphere</a> are molecules of <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/solar-system/nasas-curiosity-rover-finds-biologically-useful-nitrogen-on-mars/\">nitrogen</a> and <a href=\"https://astrobiology.nasa.gov/news/lightning-oxygen-and-life-detection/\">oxygen</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> suddenly Saturn\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-8-major-fireball-meteor/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261109/html"}